{"data":[{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.08022","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.08022","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.08022","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yao","familyName":"Liang","name":"Liang, Yao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dongcheng","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Dongcheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Feifei","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Feifei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Guobin","familyName":"Shen","name":"Shen, Guobin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuwei","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Yuwei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dongqi","familyName":"Liang","name":"Liang, Dongqi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yi","familyName":"Zeng","name":"Zeng, Yi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"DiverValue-Bench: A Benchmark and Fine-Tuning Framework for Aligning Large Language Models with Diverse Human Values"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-09T09:25:08Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-11T00:00:52Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T03:06:45Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09-17T00:18:39Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-20T09:42:41Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-08-21T00:36:20Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Aligning large language models (LLMs) with diverse human values is essential for safe and effective deployment, yet existing benchmarks often overlook cultural and demographic variation. We introduce DiverValue-Bench, a population-aware benchmark for evaluating multi-dimensional value alignment across 74 countries/regions. It contains 23,763 quality-controlled instances derived from PRISM user feedback and audited through large-scale human validation, with fine-grained value labels, personalized questions, contrastive reference answers, and rich demographic metadata. Using DiverValue-Bench, we evaluate representative LLMs and reveal substantial geographic and demographic disparities that are masked by aggregate performance. We further show that lightweight preference-based fine-tuning with Low-Rank Adaptation (LoRA) and Direct Preference Optimization (DPO) substantially improves in-domain value alignment while yielding consistent out-of-domain gains. These results highlight the need for population-aware alignment evaluation and demonstrate the utility of DiverValue-Bench as a practical foundation for global alignment, personalized value modeling, and equitable AI development."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"11 pages, 5 figures. Accepted to IJCAI-ECAI 2026 (Human-Centred AI Special Track). v2: Updated to the camera-ready version"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.08022","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-11T01:18:43Z","registered":"2025-09-11T01:18:44Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-21T02:22:06Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.08010","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.08010","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.08010","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lujain","familyName":"Ibrahim","name":"Ibrahim, Lujain","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Katherine M.","familyName":"Collins","name":"Collins, Katherine M.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sunnie S. Y.","familyName":"Kim","name":"Kim, Sunnie S. Y.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Anka","familyName":"Reuel","name":"Reuel, Anka","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Max","familyName":"Lamparth","name":"Lamparth, Max","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kevin","familyName":"Feng","name":"Feng, Kevin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lama","familyName":"Ahmad","name":"Ahmad, Lama","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Prajna","familyName":"Soni","name":"Soni, Prajna","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alia El","familyName":"Kattan","name":"Kattan, Alia El","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Merlin","familyName":"Stein","name":"Stein, Merlin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Siddharth","familyName":"Swaroop","name":"Swaroop, Siddharth","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Vishakh","familyName":"Padmakumar","name":"Padmakumar, Vishakh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ilia","familyName":"Sucholutsky","name":"Sucholutsky, Ilia","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Andrew","familyName":"Strait","name":"Strait, Andrew","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Diyi","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Diyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Q. Vera","familyName":"Liao","name":"Liao, Q. Vera","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Umang","familyName":"Bhatt","name":"Bhatt, Umang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Measuring and mitigating overreliance to build human-compatible AI"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computers and Society (cs.CY)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Human-Computer Interaction (cs.HC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-08T16:15:07Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-11T00:00:38Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-05-20T11:59:51Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-05-21T00:55:03Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large language models (LLMs) distinguish themselves from previous technologies by functioning as collaborative ``thought partners,'' capable of engaging more fluidly in natural language on a range of tasks. As LLMs increasingly influence consequential decisions across diverse domains from healthcare to personal advice, the risk of overreliance -- relying on LLMs beyond their capabilities -- grows. This paper argues that measuring and mitigating overreliance must become central to LLM research and deployment. First, we consolidate risks from overreliance at both the individual and societal levels, including high-stakes errors, governance challenges, and cognitive deskilling. Then, we explore LLM characteristics, system design features, and user cognitive biases that together raise serious and unique concerns about overreliance on LLMs in practice. We also examine historical approaches for measuring overreliance, identifying three important gaps and proposing three promising directions to improve measurement. Finally, we propose mitigation strategies that can be pursued to ensure LLMs augment rather than undermine human capabilities."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.08010","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-11T01:18:26Z","registered":"2025-09-11T01:18:27Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-05-21T02:34:20Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.08031","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.08031","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.08031","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hoang","familyName":"Nguyen","name":"Nguyen, Hoang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sidharth","familyName":"Surapaneni","name":"Surapaneni, Sidharth","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Akshay","familyName":"Kalkunte","name":"Kalkunte, Akshay","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jash","familyName":"Mehta","name":"Mehta, Jash","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Aman","familyName":"Tiwari","name":"Tiwari, Aman","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Oluwanifemi","familyName":"Bamgbose","name":"Bamgbose, Oluwanifemi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Khyati","familyName":"Mahajan","name":"Mahajan, Khyati","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jash","familyName":"Shah","name":"Shah, Jash","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shruthan","familyName":"Radhakrishna","name":"Radhakrishna, Shruthan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sathwik Tejaswi","familyName":"Madhusudhan","name":"Madhusudhan, Sathwik Tejaswi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Vikas","familyName":"Yadav","name":"Yadav, Vikas","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sai","familyName":"Rajeswar","name":"Rajeswar, Sai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"AU-Harness: An Open-Source Toolkit for Holistic Evaluation of Audio LLMs"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Sound (cs.SD)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Audio and Speech Processing (eess.AS)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-09T15:30:40Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-11T00:01:05Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-11T16:27:59Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09-12T00:45:29Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-05-11T14:29:23Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-05-12T02:17:29Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large Audio Language Models (LALMs) are rapidly advancing, but evaluating them remains challenging due to inefficient and non-standardized toolkits that limit fair comparison and systematic assessment. Existing evaluation frameworks exhibit three critical limitations: (1) slow and inefficient processing pipeline that bottlenecks large-scale studies, (2) inadequate multi-turn dialogue support, leaving fundamental questions about cross-turn context integration and performance dynamics over extended conversations in LALMs unanswered; and (3) the absence of unified and scalable evaluation framework capable of keeping pace with the rapid growth of both LALMs and audio benchmarks. To address these issues, we introduce AU-Harness, an efficient and comprehensive evaluation framework for LALMs. Our system achieves a speedup of up to 151% over existing evaluation toolkits through optimized batch processing and parallel execution, enabling large-scale evaluations previously considered impractical. We provide standardized prompting protocols and flexible configurations for fair model comparison across diverse scenarios. AU-Harness unlocks a range of in-depth analyses difficult to conduct without a unified foundation, including multi-turn dialogue dynamics, enabling the study of true audio reasoning capabilities in existing LALMs. AU-Harness provides both practical evaluation tools and insights into model limitations, advancing systematic LALM development."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.08031","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-11T01:18:56Z","registered":"2025-09-11T01:18:56Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-05-12T05:02:38Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.08016","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.08016","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.08016","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hyungjin","familyName":"Chung","name":"Chung, Hyungjin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hyelin","familyName":"Nam","name":"Nam, Hyelin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiyeon","familyName":"Kim","name":"Kim, Jiyeon","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hyojun","familyName":"Go","name":"Go, Hyojun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Byeongjun","familyName":"Park","name":"Park, Byeongjun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Junho","familyName":"Kim","name":"Kim, Junho","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Joonseok","familyName":"Lee","name":"Lee, Joonseok","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Seongsu","familyName":"Ha","name":"Ha, Seongsu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Byung-Hoon","familyName":"Kim","name":"Kim, Byung-Hoon","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Video Parallel Scaling: Aggregating Diverse Frame Subsets for VideoLLMs"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-09T00:55:04Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-11T00:00:46Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-04-09T01:22:20Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-04-10T00:17:44Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Video Large Language Models (VideoLLMs) face a critical bottleneck: increasing the number of input frames to capture fine-grained temporal detail leads to prohibitive computational costs and performance degradation from long context lengths. We introduce Video Parallel Scaling (VPS), an inference-time method that expands a model's perceptual bandwidth without increasing its context window. VPS operates by running multiple parallel inference streams, each processing a unique, disjoint subset of the video's frames. By aggregating the output probabilities from these complementary streams, VPS integrates a richer set of visual information than is possible with a single pass. We theoretically show that this approach effectively contracts the Chinchilla scaling law by leveraging uncorrelated visual evidence, thereby improving performance without additional training. Extensive experiments across various model architectures and scales (2B-32B) on benchmarks such as Video-MME and EventHallusion demonstrate that VPS consistently and significantly improves performance. It scales more favorably than other parallel alternatives (e.g. Self-consistency) and is complementary to other decoding strategies, offering a memory-efficient and robust framework for enhancing the temporal reasoning capabilities of VideoLLMs."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"CVPR Findings 2026; code: https://github.com/hyungjin-chung/VPS"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.08016","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-11T01:18:35Z","registered":"2025-09-11T01:18:36Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-04-10T02:23:57Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.08829","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.08829","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.08829","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chandan Kumar","familyName":"Sah","name":"Sah, Chandan Kumar","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"PerFairX: Is There a Balance Between Fairness and Personality in Large Language Model Recommendations?"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computers and Society (cs.CY)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Information Retrieval (cs.IR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-08-20T09:41:53Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-12T00:00:03Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The integration of Large Language Models (LLMs) into recommender systems has enabled zero-shot, personality-based personalization through prompt-based interactions, offering a new paradigm for user-centric recommendations. However, incorporating user personality traits via the OCEAN model highlights a critical tension between achieving psychological alignment and ensuring demographic fairness. To address this, we propose PerFairX, a unified evaluation framework designed to quantify the trade-offs between personalization and demographic equity in LLM-generated recommendations. Using neutral and personality-sensitive prompts across diverse user profiles, we benchmark two state-of-the-art LLMs, ChatGPT and DeepSeek, on movie (MovieLens 10M) and music (Last.fm 360K) datasets. Our results reveal that personality-aware prompting significantly improves alignment with individual traits but can exacerbate fairness disparities across demographic groups. Specifically, DeepSeek achieves stronger psychological fit but exhibits higher sensitivity to prompt variations, while ChatGPT delivers stable yet less personalized outputs. PerFairX provides a principled benchmark to guide the development of LLM-based recommender systems that are both equitable and psychologically informed, contributing to the creation of inclusive, user-centric AI applications in continual learning contexts."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"10 pages, 5 figures. Accepted to the Workshop on Multimodal Continual Learning (MCL) at ICCV 2025. @2025 IEEE/CVF International Conference on Computer Vision Workshops (ICCVW), ICCV's 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.08829","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-12T01:19:34Z","registered":"2025-09-12T01:19:34Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-12T01:19:34Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.08750","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.08750","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.08750","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuanchun","familyName":"Guo","name":"Guo, Yuanchun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bingyan","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Bingyan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yulong","familyName":"Sha","name":"Sha, Yulong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhensheng","familyName":"Xian","name":"Xian, Zhensheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"PracMHBench: Re-evaluating Model-Heterogeneous Federated Learning Based on Practical Edge Device Constraints"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Distributed, Parallel, and Cluster Computing (cs.DC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-04T08:19:56Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-11T00:44:06Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Federating heterogeneous models on edge devices with diverse resource constraints has been a notable trend in recent years. Compared to traditional federated learning (FL) that assumes an identical model architecture to cooperate, model-heterogeneous FL is more practical and flexible since the model can be customized to satisfy the deployment requirement. Unfortunately, no prior work ever dives into the existing model-heterogeneous FL algorithms under the practical edge device constraints and provides quantitative analysis on various data scenarios and metrics, which motivates us to rethink and re-evaluate this paradigm. In our work, we construct the first system platform \\textbf{PracMHBench} to evaluate model-heterogeneous FL on practical constraints of edge devices, where diverse model heterogeneity algorithms are classified and tested on multiple data tasks and metrics. Based on the platform, we perform extensive experiments on these algorithms under the different edge constraints to observe their applicability and the corresponding heterogeneity pattern."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted by DAC2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.08750","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-11T01:36:03Z","registered":"2025-09-11T01:36:03Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-11T01:36:03Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.08697","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.08697","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.08697","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"James","familyName":"Gong","name":"Gong, James","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Raymond","familyName":"Luo","name":"Luo, Raymond","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Emma","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Emma","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Leon","familyName":"Ge","name":"Ge, Leon","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bruce","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Bruce","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Felix","familyName":"Marattukalam","name":"Marattukalam, Felix","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Waleed","familyName":"Abdulla","name":"Abdulla, Waleed","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Reshaping the Forward-Forward Algorithm with a Similarity-Based Objective"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-08-29T10:23:03Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-11T00:41:38Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Backpropagation is the pivotal algorithm underpinning the success of artificial neural networks, yet it has critical limitations such as biologically implausible backward locking and global error propagation. To circumvent these constraints, the Forward-Forward algorithm was proposed as a more biologically plausible method that replaces the backward pass with an additional forward pass. Despite this advantage, the Forward-Forward algorithm significantly trails backpropagation in accuracy, and its optimal form exhibits low inference efficiency due to multiple forward passes required. In this work, the Forward-Forward algorithm is reshaped through its integration with similarity learning frameworks, eliminating the need for multiple forward passes during inference. This proposed algorithm is named Forward-Forward Algorithm Unified with Similarity-based Tuplet loss (FAUST). Empirical evaluations on MNIST, Fashion-MNIST, and CIFAR-10 datasets indicate that FAUST substantially improves accuracy, narrowing the gap with backpropagation. On CIFAR-10, FAUST achieves 56.22\\% accuracy with a simple multi-layer perceptron architecture, approaching the backpropagation benchmark of 57.63\\% accuracy."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"6 pages"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.08697","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-11T01:34:48Z","registered":"2025-09-11T01:34:48Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-11T01:34:48Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.08008","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.08008","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.08008","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bingjian","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Bingjian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Danni","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Danni","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kaipeng","familyName":"Niu","name":"Niu, Kaipeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wenxuan","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Wenxuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zheng","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Zheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mohan","familyName":"Kankanhalli","name":"Kankanhalli, Mohan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"A New Dataset and Benchmark for Grounding Multimodal Misinformation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Social and Information Networks (cs.SI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Multimedia (cs.MM)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-08T10:56:07Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-11T00:00:36Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1145/3746027.3758191","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The proliferation of online misinformation videos poses serious societal risks. Current datasets and detection methods primarily target binary classification or single-modality localization based on post-processed data, lacking the interpretability needed to counter persuasive misinformation. In this paper, we introduce the task of Grounding Multimodal Misinformation (GroundMM), which verifies multimodal content and localizes misleading segments across modalities. We present the first real-world dataset for this task, GroundLie360, featuring a taxonomy of misinformation types, fine-grained annotations across text, speech, and visuals, and validation with Snopes evidence and annotator reasoning. We also propose a VLM-based, QA-driven baseline, FakeMark, using single- and cross-modal cues for effective detection and grounding. Our experiments highlight the challenges of this task and lay a foundation for explainable multimodal misinformation detection."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"6 pages, 5 figures, ACM Multimedia 2025 Dataset Track"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.08008","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-09-11T01:18:23Z","registered":"2025-09-11T01:18:24Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-11T01:18:24Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.08004","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.08004","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.08004","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zoya","familyName":"Hammad","name":"Hammad, Zoya","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nii Longdon","familyName":"Sowah","name":"Sowah, Nii Longdon","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Evaluating and comparing gender bias across four text-to-image models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computers and Society (cs.CY)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-07T22:15:58Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-11T00:00:31Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"As we increasingly use Artificial Intelligence (AI) in decision-making for industries like healthcare, finance, e-commerce, and even entertainment, it is crucial to also reflect on the ethical aspects of AI, for example the inclusivity and fairness of the information it provides. In this work, we aimed to evaluate different text-to-image AI models and compare the degree of gender bias they present. The evaluated models were Stable Diffusion XL (SDXL), Stable Diffusion Cascade (SC), DALL-E and Emu. We hypothesized that DALL-E and Stable Diffusion, which are comparatively older models, would exhibit a noticeable degree of gender bias towards men, while Emu, which was recently released by Meta AI, would have more balanced results. As hypothesized, we found that both Stable Diffusion models exhibit a noticeable degree of gender bias while Emu demonstrated more balanced results (i.e. less gender bias). However, interestingly, Open AI's DALL-E exhibited almost opposite results, such that the ratio of women to men was significantly higher in most cases tested. Here, although we still observed a bias, the bias favored females over males. This bias may be explained by the fact that OpenAI changed the prompts at its backend, as observed during our experiment. We also observed that Emu from Meta AI utilized user information while generating images via WhatsApp. We also proposed some potential solutions to avoid such biases, including ensuring diversity across AI research teams and having diverse datasets."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.08004","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-11T01:18:18Z","registered":"2025-09-11T01:18:18Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-11T01:18:18Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.08000","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.08000","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.08000","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Debdeep","familyName":"Sanyal","name":"Sanyal, Debdeep","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Manodeep","familyName":"Ray","name":"Ray, Manodeep","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Murari","familyName":"Mandal","name":"Mandal, Murari","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"AntiDote: Bi-level Adversarial Training for Tamper-Resistant LLMs"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-06T16:03:07Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-11T00:00:27Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The release of open-weight large language models (LLMs) creates a tension between advancing accessible research and preventing misuse, such as malicious fine-tuning to elicit harmful content. Current safety measures struggle to preserve the general capabilities of the LLM while resisting a determined adversary with full access to the model's weights and architecture, who can use full-parameter fine-tuning to erase existing safeguards. To address this, we introduce AntiDote, a bi-level optimization procedure for training LLMs to be resistant to such tampering. AntiDote involves an auxiliary adversary hypernetwork that learns to generate malicious Low-Rank Adaptation (LoRA) weights conditioned on the defender model's internal activations. The defender LLM is then trained with an objective to nullify the effect of these adversarial weight additions, forcing it to maintain its safety alignment. We validate this approach against a diverse suite of 52 red-teaming attacks, including jailbreak prompting, latent space manipulation, and direct weight-space attacks. AntiDote is upto 27.4\\% more robust against adversarial attacks compared to both tamper-resistance and unlearning baselines. Crucially, this robustness is achieved with a minimal trade-off in utility, incurring a performance degradation of upto less than 0.5\\% across capability benchmarks including MMLU, HellaSwag, and GSM8K. Our work offers a practical and compute efficient methodology for building open-weight models where safety is a more integral and resilient property."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"19 pages"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.08000","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-11T01:18:12Z","registered":"2025-09-11T01:18:13Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-11T01:18:13Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}}],"meta":{"total":10,"totalPages":1,"page":1},"links":{"self":"https://api.datacite.org/dois?client-id=arxiv.content\u0026page%5Bsize%5D=100\u0026query=doi%3A10.48550%2Farxiv.2509.08000+OR+doi%3A10.48550%2Farxiv.2509.08004+OR+doi%3A10.48550%2Farxiv.2509.08008+OR+doi%3A10.48550%2Farxiv.2509.08010+OR+doi%3A10.48550%2Farxiv.2509.08016+OR+doi%3A10.48550%2Farxiv.2509.08022+OR+doi%3A10.48550%2Farxiv.2509.08031+OR+doi%3A10.48550%2Farxiv.2509.08697+OR+doi%3A10.48550%2Farxiv.2509.08750+OR+doi%3A10.48550%2Farxiv.2509.08829"}}